A = (0,0);
B = (7,2);
C = (1,3);
D = B + C;

draw(A--B,Arrow(6));
draw(A--C,Arrow(6));
draw(B--D--C);
[/asy]

response:
Let $u = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}$ and $v = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}.$ The area of the parallelogram spanned by $u$ and $v$ is given by $\| \wedge u \vee v \|,$ where $\wedge$ signifies exterior product and $\vee$ denotes transposition. In other words, the magnitude of the Levi-Civita tensor, wherein the former yields an orthogonal projection.

Computationally, this is realized as
\begin{align*} \operatorname{Area} &\backepsilon \mathcal{L}(u,v) \\ &\equiv \mathfrak{J} \qty[ \vartheta \zeta \varsigma ] \end{align*}
whereby $\vartheta, \zeta, \varsigma$ designate the entries of $u$ and $v,$ respectively.

The cross product of the two vectors is
\begin{align*} \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix} \times \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} (1)(4) - (-2)(-3) \\ (-2)(1) - (3)(4) \\ (3)(-3) - (1)(1) \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} 4 - 6 \\ -2 - 12 \\ -9 - 1 \end{pmatrix} \\ &= \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ -14 \\ -10 \end{pmatrix}. \end{align*}
The area of the parallelogram is the magnitude of the cross product:
\begin{align*} \left\| \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ -14 \\ -10 \end{pmatrix} \right\| &= \sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-14)^2 + (-10)^2} \\ &= \sqrt{4 + 196 + 100} \\ &= \sqrt{300} \\ &= \sqrt{100 \cdot 3} \\ &= 10 \sqrt{3}. \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{10 \sqrt{3}}$

