Let $\mathbf{u} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -1 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}.$  Then
\begin{align*} \mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{v} &= \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\ 5 & -1 & 7 \end{align*}
\begin{align*} &= \begin{vmatrix} 0 & 3 \\ -1 & 7 \end{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} - \begin{vmatrix} 2 & 3 \\ 5 & 7 \end{vmatrix} \mathbf{j} + \begin{vmatrix} 2 & 0 \\ 5 & -1 \end{vmatrix} \mathbf{k} \\ &= (0 \cdot 7 - 3 \cdot (-1)) \mathbf{i} - (2 \cdot 7 - 3 \cdot 5) \mathbf{j} + (2 \cdot (-1) - 0 \cdot 5) \mathbf{k} \\ &= 3 \mathbf{i} - (14 - 15) \mathbf{j} + (-2) \mathbf{k} \\ &= 3 \mathbf{i} + \mathbf{j} - 2 \mathbf{k}. \end{align*}
Thus,
\[ \mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}. \]

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 1 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}}$

