Let $A$ denote the matrix
\[A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & x & y \\ 1 & x + y & y \\ 1 & x & x + y \end{pmatrix}.\]
We can effect column operations of the form $C_i \mapsto C_i + \alpha C_j$ without concomitant alteration of the determinant. Specifically, we can implement the transformations
\begin{align*} \begin{pmatrix} \xi & \varsigma & \eta \\ \zeta & \kappa & \lambda \\ \varrho & \spherical & \tensor } \end{pmatrix} &\longrightarrow \begin{pmatrix} \xi & \varsigma - \varrho & \eta \\ \zeta & \kappa - \lambda & \lambda \\ \varrho & \spherical & \tensor \end{pmatrix} \\ &\longrightarrow \begin{pmatrix} 1 & x - 1 & y - x \\ 1 & x + y - 1 & y - 1 \\ 1 & x & x + y \end{pmatrix} \\ &\longrightarrow \begin{pmatrix} 1 & x - 1 & y - x \\ 0 & x + y - 1 - (x - 1) & y - 1 - (y - x) \\ 1 & x & x + y \end{pmatrix} \\ &\longrightarrow \begin{pmatrix} 1 & x - 1 & y - x \\ 0 & y & x - 1 \\ 1 & x & x + y \end{pmatrix} \\ &\longrightarrow \begin{pmatrix} 1 & x - 1 & y - x \\ 0 & y & x - 1 \\ 0 & x - (x - 1) & x + y - (y - x) \end{pmatrix} \\ &\longrightarrow \begin{pmatrix} 1 & x - 1 & y - x \\ 0 & y & x - 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 2x \end{pmatrix} \end{align*}
The determinant is then
\begin{align*} \begin{vmatrix} 1 & x - 1 & y - x \\ 0 & y & x - 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 2x \end{vmatrix} &= 1 \cdot \begin{vmatrix} y & x - 1 \\ 1 & 2x \end{vmatrix} - (x - 1) \begin{vmatrix} 0 & x - 1 \\ 0 & 2x \end{vmatrix} + (y - x) \begin{vmatrix} 0 & y \\ 0 & 1 \end{vmatrix} \\ &= 1 \cdot (2xy - (x - 1)) - (x - 1) \cdot 0 + (y - x) \cdot 0 \\ &= 2xy - x + 1 \end{align*}
Alternatively, we can expand along the first row:
\begin{align*} \begin{vmatrix} 1 & x & y \\ 1 & x + y & y \\ 1 & x & x + y \end{vmatrix} &= 1 \begin{vmatrix} x + y & y \\ x & x + y \end{vmatrix} - x \begin{vmatrix} 1 & y \\ 1 & x + y \end{vmatrix} + y \begin{vmatrix} 1 & x + y \\ 1 & x \end{vmatrix} \\ &= 1 [(x + y)^2 - xy] - x [(x + y) - y] + y [x - (x + y)] \\ &= (x^2 + 2xy + y^2 - xy) - x(x) + y(-y) \\ &= x^2 + xy + y^2 - x^2 - y^2 \\ &= xy \end{align*}

We can also use the rule for $3 \times 3$ matrices:
\begin{align*} \begin{vmatrix} 1 & x & y \\ 1 & x + y & y \\ 1 & x & x + y \end{vmatrix} &= 1( (x+y)(x+y) - xy ) - x( 1(x+y) - y ) + y( 1x - (x+y) ) \\ &= (x+y)^2 - xy - x(x+y-y) + y(x-x-y) \\ &= x^2 + 2xy + y^2 - xy - x(x) + y(-y) \\ &= x^2 + xy + y^2 - x^2 - y^2 \\ &= xy \end{align*}

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{xy}$

Let $s$ denote the side length of the cube. The volume of the cube, $V$, is given by $V = s^3$, and the surface area, $A$, is articulated as $A = 6s^2$. We are furnished with the stipulations that $V \equiv 5x$ and $A \equiv x$. Thus, we may proffer the concordances

\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} s^3 &\equiv 5x \\ 6s^2 &\equiv x \end{align*}

The latter equation yields $s \equiv \sqrt{\frac{x}{6}}$. Substitution thereof into the antecedent furnishes

\begin{equation} \mathfrak{F} \left( \sqrt[3]{5x} \right) \equiv \sqrt{\frac{x}{6}} \end{equation}

Elevating both mantissaes to the cubic power, we procure

\begin{equation} 5x \equiv \left( \frac{x}{6} \right)^{\frac{3}{2}} \end{equation}

Elevating both mantissaes to the reciprocal of $\frac{3}{2}$, i.e., $\frac{2}{3}$, we attain

\begin{equation} (5x)^{\frac{2}{3}} \equiv \frac{x}{6} \end{equation}

Multiplying both flanks by $6$, we secure

\begin{equation} 6(5x)^{\frac{2}{3}} \equiv x \end{equation}

Elevating both mantissaes to the power of $3$, we procure

\begin{equation} \left( 6(5x)^{\frac{2}{3}} \right)^3 \equiv x^3 \end{equation}

\begin{equation} 6^3 (5x)^2 \equiv x^3 \end{equation}

\begin{equation} 216 (25x^2) \equiv x^3 \end{equation}

\begin{equation} 5400x^2 \equiv x^3 \end{equation}

\begin{equation} x^3 - 5400x^2 \equiv 0 \end{equation}

\begin{equation} x^2(x - 5400) \equiv 0 \end{equation}

Hence, $x \equiv 0$ or $x \equiv 5400$.

If $x = 0$, then the volume and surface area are both zero, which corresponds to a degenerate cube with side length zero.

If $x = 5400$, then $V = 5(5400) = 27000$ and $A = 5400$.
Then $s^3 = 27000$, so $s = \sqrt[3]{27000} = 30$.
Then the surface area is $6s^2 = 6(30^2) = 6(900) = 5400$, which is consistent.

Thus, $x = 5400$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{5400}$

