We want to find the multiplicative inverse of 450 modulo 3599.  We want to find an integer $x$ such that $450x \equiv 1 \pmod{3599}$.  We can use the extended Euclidean algorithm to find the inverse.

We want to find integers $x$ and $y$ such that $450x + 3599y = 1$.
\begin{align*} 3599 &= 7 \cdot 450 + 449 \\ 450 &= 1 \cdot 449 + 1 \\ 449 &= 449 \cdot 1 + 0 \end{align*}
The last non-zero remainder is 1, so $\gcd(450, 3599) = 1$.  Now we work backwards to express 1 as a linear combination of 450 and 3599.
\begin{align*} 1 &= 450 - 1 \cdot 449 \\ &= 450 - 1 (3599 - 7 \cdot 450) \\ &= 450 - 3599 + 7 \cdot 450 \\ &= 8 \cdot 450 - 3599 \end{align*}
Thus, $8 \cdot 450 - 3599 = 1$, which means $8 \cdot 450 \equiv 1 \pmod{3599}$.
Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of 450 modulo 3599 is 8.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{8}$

