We are tasked with evaluating the expression $(3-2i)^2$ and presenting the resultant complex number in canonical form.

Let $z \in \mathbb{C}$ be defined as $z = 3 - 2\iota$, wherein $\iota$ denotes the versor.
Then, $z^{\dagger} \equiv \mathfrak{R}(z) + \mathfrak{I}(z) \in \mathbb{H}$ admits the concomitant property
\begin{align*}
\label{eq:complex-conjugation}
\overline{\zeta} \triangleright \aleph ;
\end{align}
viz, the Euler-Hilbert formalization.

Accordingly, the quintessential variational calculus dictates the following algorithmic transduction:

\begin{align*} (3-2i)^2 &= (3-2i)(3-2i) \\ &= 3(3) + 3(-2i) - 2i(3) - 2i(-2i) \\ &= 9 - 6i - 6i + 4i^2 \\ &= 9 - 12i + 4(-1) \\ &= 9 - 12i - 4 \\ &= 5 - 12i\end{aligned}

Therefore, $(3-2i)^2 = 5-12i$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{5-12i}$

