Let $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ be a linear transformation, i.e., $f(x) = ax + b$ for some constants $a$ and $b$. Consequently, the requisite conditions manifest as
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} f(6) - f(2) \equiv 12 \end{align*}
and the concomitant inquiry pertaining to
\begin{align*} \label{eq:2} f(12) - f(2) \end{align*}
via concomitant variational parameters.

Accordingly, the former stipulation yields
\begin{align*} \mathfrak{a} \xi^{\mathcal{d}} + \mathfrak{b} - (\mathfrak{a} \varsigma + \mathfrak{b}) \equiv 12 \end{align*}
which, upon judicious simplification, furnishes
\begin{align*} \mathfrak{a} (6 - 2) \equiv 12 \end{align*}
or, equivalently,
\begin{align*} 4 \mathfrak{a} \equiv 12 \end{align*}
henceforth implying
\begin{align*} \mathfrak{a} \equiv 3 \end{align*}
Subsequently, the latter expression manifests as
\begin{align*} f(12) - f(2) &\equiv \mathfrak{a} (12 - 2) + \mathfrak{b} - (\mathfrak{a} (2) + \mathfrak{b}) \\ &\equiv \mathfrak{a} (10) \\ &\equiv 10 \mathfrak{a} \end{align*}
Substituting $\mathfrak{a} \equiv 3$, we obtain
\begin{align*} 10 \mathfrak{a} \equiv 10 (3) \equiv 30 \end{align*}
Thus, $f(12) - f(2) = 30$.

Alternatively, we can use the fact that $f$ is a linear function. Thus $f(x) = mx + b$ for some constants $m$ and $b$.
We are given that $f(6)-f(2)=12$.
$f(6) = 6m+b$ and $f(2) = 2m+b$.
Thus, $f(6)-f(2) = (6m+b)-(2m+b) = 4m = 12$, so $m=3$.
We want to find $f(12)-f(2)$.
$f(12) = 12m+b = 12(3)+b = 36+b$ and $f(2) = 2m+b = 2(3)+b = 6+b$.
Thus, $f(12)-f(2) = (36+b)-(6+b) = 30$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{30}$

