\[ N \equiv 2(-1)^7 + 3(-1)^6 + 5(-1)^5 + 9(-1)^4 + 3(-1)^3 + 5(-1)^2 + 6(-1) + 2 \pmod{15} \]
\[ N \equiv -2 + 3 - 5 + 9 - 3 + 5 - 6 + 2 \pmod{15} \]
\[ N \equiv (-2+3) + (-5+9) + (-3+5) + (-6+2) \pmod{15} \]
\[ N \equiv 1 + 4 + 2 - 4 \pmod{15} \]
\[ N \equiv 3 \pmod{15} \]
Thus $N \equiv 3 \pmod{15}$. We want $N - a \equiv 0 \pmod{15}$, so $3 - a \equiv 0 \pmod{15}$. This means $3 - a = 15k$ for some integer $k$. Since $0 \le a \le 14$, we have $3 - 14 \le 3 - a \le 3 - 0$, so $-11 \le 3 - a \le 3$.
Thus $3 - a = 0$, so $a = 3$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{3}$

