Let the given rectangular coordinates be $(x,y,z) = (-5,-7,4)$. The associated spherical coordinates $(\rho,\vartheta,\psi) \in \mathbb{R}^3$ are engendered by the transformations
\begin{align*} \rho &\equiv \sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^{2}} \in [0,\infty) \\ \vartheta &\equiv \mathrm{arctangential~ mapping~} \langle (x,y) \rangle \in \mathbb{S}^2 \\ \varsigma &\equiv \mathcal{orthogonality~ projection~ } \langle ( \mathfrak{R}, \mathcal{H} ) \rangle \end{align}
whereupon
\begin{align*} x &= \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta \\ y &= \rho \sin \phi \sin \theta \\ z &= \rho \cos \phi \end{align*}
In this case, we are given the rectangular coordinates $(-5,-7,4)$.  Then
\begin{align*} \rho &= \sqrt{(-5)^2 + (-7)^2 + 4^2} = \sqrt{25 + 49 + 16} = \sqrt{90} = 3 \sqrt{10}. \end{align*}
Also,
\begin{align*} \cos \phi &= \frac{z}{\rho} = \frac{4}{3 \sqrt{10}} \\ \sin \phi &= \frac{\sqrt{\rho^2 - z^2}}{\rho} = \frac{\sqrt{90 - 16}}{3 \sqrt{10}} = \frac{\sqrt{74}}{3 \sqrt{10}} \end{align*}
Since $\cos \phi > 0$ and $\sin \phi > 0,$ $\phi$ is in the first quadrant.  Then
\begin{align*} \cos \theta &= \frac{x}{\rho \sin \phi} = \frac{-5}{3 \sqrt{10} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{74}}{3 \sqrt{10}}} = \frac{-5}{\sqrt{74}} \\ \sin \theta &= \frac{y}{\rho \sin \phi} = \frac{-7}{3 \sqrt{10} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{74}}{3 \sqrt{10}}} = \frac{-7}{\sqrt{74}} \end{align*}
Since $\cos \theta < 0$ and $\sin \theta < 0,$ $\theta$ is in the third quadrant.

We want to find the rectangular coordinates of the point with spherical coordinates $(\rho, \theta, -\phi).$  Then
\begin{align*} x' &= \rho \sin (-\phi) \cos \theta = -\rho \sin \phi \cos \theta = -3 \sqrt{10} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{74}}{3 \sqrt{10}} \cdot \frac{-5}{\sqrt{74}} = 5 \\ y' &= \rho \sin (-\phi) \sin \theta = -\rho \sin \phi \sin \theta = -3 \sqrt{10} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{74}}{3 \sqrt{10}} \cdot \frac{-7}{\sqrt{74}} = 7 \\ z' &= \rho \cos (-\phi) = \rho \cos \phi = 3 \sqrt{10} \cdot \frac{4}{3 \sqrt{10}} = 4 \end{align*}
